@packages/compiler/src/evaluation-execution.sigil import { SigilAgentExecutionPolicy }

component SigilAgentAdapterExecutionCoordinator {
  goal {
    Coordinate provider-neutral execution settlement, resource observation,
    terminal arbitration, and cleanup verification without owning provider
    invocation details.
  }

  interface {
    AdapterExecutionHooks {
      preflight(serializedInput, AgentOperationalLimits) returns success or
      operational-limit without starting provider execution

      invoke(registerDeclaration, reportObservation, submitTerminalCondition) is
      supplied by the adapter implementation that owns provider-specific mechanics;
      the coordinator atomically linearizes its final cancellation observation with
      calling it at most once and observes its submissions

      cleanup(winningCondition, providerCleanupMs, reportObservation) performs
      implementation-specific cleanup attempts and reports their outcomes

      every owned resource and result input is registered before first use or state
      observation
    }

    AdapterExecutionDeclaration {
      identity is stable and unique within one evaluation; ownership means the
      implementation created the resource or accepted cleanup responsibility

      resource lifecycle state is active, terminal, or released; result-input
      lifecycle state is open, closed, or cancelled; latest known state is initially
      absent

      observation status is observed, failed, incomplete, or impossible and is
      recorded separately from latest known lifecycle state

      an observed state updates latest known state monotonically; terminal, released,
      closed, and cancelled never regress

      failed, incomplete, or impossible observation records evidence without
      overwriting latest known state; final terminal or released proof remains valid,
      while absent or nonterminal latest state cannot verify cleanup
    }

    AdapterTerminalArbiter {
      submit(condition) atomically appends one eligible terminal condition with a
      monotonic evaluation-local sequence

      the first submission while idle schedules one deferred drain; at drain start,
      one atomic snapshot takes every submitted condition and immediately closes the
      batch; later submissions belong to the next batch

      within one batch, kind precedence is cancellation, elapsed-time,
      preventive-budget, operational-limit, execution, final-result-protocol, then
      newly eligible valid result; the lowest sequence wins within one kind

      a second valid-result submission becomes final-result-protocol at the second
      submission's sequence; an already settled valid result is immutable
    }

    CoordinatorResult {
      contains exactly one provider-produced evaluation result envelope after
      execution and declared result-input settlement
    }

    CoordinatorFailure {
      kind is cancelled, elapsed-time, preventive-budget, operational-limit,
      execution, final-result-protocol, or cleanup

      cleanup contains complete recovery evidence and the initiating terminal kind
    }

    AgentAdapterExecutionCoordinator {
      execute(AdapterExecutionHooks, SigilAgentExecutionPolicy) returns one
      CoordinatorResult or one CoordinatorFailure

      a non-result winner invokes cleanup; cleanup is verified only when every
      declared resource has latest known terminal or released state and every result
      input has latest known closed or cancelled state before the cleanup deadline

      cleanup recovery evidence contains all declarations and latest states,
      observation failures, ordered attempts, deadline outcome, initiating terminal
      kind, and operator recovery action
    }
  }
}

expand SigilAgentAdapterExecutionCoordinator {
  logic {
    AgentAdapterExecutionCoordinator {
      Run hook preflight before invocation. After invocation, accept only registered
      state observations and eligible terminal submissions. If the shared monotonic
      elapsed deadline expires after invocation, submit one elapsed-time terminal
      condition.

      At the invocation transition, atomically observe cancellation and start the
      provider. Cancellation observed before the transition returns cancelled without
      invocation; cancellation observed after it becomes an eligible terminal
      submission.

      At each deferred drain, atomically snapshot submitted conditions and apply kind
      precedence, then lowest evaluation-local sequence. A condition submitted during
      or after the snapshot enters the next batch.

      A valid result is eligible only after execution and all result inputs settle
      and its envelope validates. Operational-limit is eligible when provider framing
      crosses maxProviderFrameChars. Final-result-protocol is eligible only after
      execution and result-input settlement without one valid envelope.

      A non-result winner starts cleanup. Earlier failed attempts do not prevent
      verification when final latest known states satisfy the contract before the
      deadline.

      Deadline expiry with an absent, active, open, or otherwise nonterminal latest
      state, or inability to obtain required proof, returns cleanup. A later
      observation failure does not erase prior immutable terminal proof but remains
      in recovery evidence.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    CoordinatorBoundary {
      Own provider-neutral declaration, batching, precedence, verification, and
      outcome policy. Adapter implementations own invocation and cleanup mechanisms
      and supply only hooks and observations.

      Never accept an undeclared resource or result-input observation. Never accept a
      late result after a non-result winner or a result settled in an earlier batch.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    CancellationInvocationLinearization {
      Decision: Atomically linearize provider invocation with a final cancellation
      observation after adapter and hook preflight checks.

      Scope: Governs cancellation arriving after the initial adapter cancellation
      check and around provider invocation.

      Trade-offs: The invocation transition requires one serialized host operation.

      Design issues addressed: An ungoverned preflight-to-invocation gap permits the
      same cancellation race to either start or avoid provider side effects.

      Consequences: Cancellation before the transition invokes no provider, while
      cancellation after it participates in coordinator arbitration and cleanup.
    }

    DeterministicTerminalArbitration {
      Decision: Use deferred atomic snapshots, fixed kind precedence, and lowest
      evaluation-local sequence within one kind.

      Scope: Governs post-invocation terminal settlement before cleanup.

      Trade-offs: A stop or failure in the same snapshot rejects a newly eligible
      result, while a result settled in an earlier snapshot remains final.

      Design issues addressed: Cancellation reflects caller intent; hard time and
      budget bounds precede provider defects; framing limits precede execution and
      terminal protocol interpretation.

      Discarded alternatives: Provider arrival order, result-first snapshots,
      aggregation, and implementation iteration order were rejected.

      Consequences: Equivalent submitted conditions produce one stable outcome.
    }

    CleanupFailureSupersession {
      Decision: Return cleanup instead of the initiating failure when cleanup cannot
      be verified, while retaining the initiating kind and recovery evidence.

      Scope: Governs cleanup after a non-result winner.

      Trade-offs: Callers inspect evidence to recover the initiating failure.

      Design issues addressed: Preserving only the initiating kind would hide a
      potentially live or unobserved provider resource.

      Discarded alternatives: Optional cleanup detail and treating deadline expiry as
      successful cleanup were rejected.

      Consequences: Failed, timed-out, indeterminate, or incompletely observed
      cleanup is compatibility-visible.
    }
  }

  cases {
    AgentAdapterExecutionCoordinator {
      A signal submitted before an atomic snapshot participates in that batch; one
      submitted during or after the snapshot enters the next batch.

      Same-kind failures choose the lowest sequence. A second valid-result submission
      becomes final-result-protocol and returns no result.

      A newly eligible result sharing a batch with cancellation returns cancelled;
      without cancellation but with elapsed-time it returns elapsed-time; without
      either but with framing overflow it returns operational-limit.

      Elapsed-time expiry submitted before the snapshot defeats a newly eligible
      result in that snapshot; a valid result settled in an earlier snapshot remains
      immutable when the timer submission arrives later.

      Cancellation arriving immediately before the atomic invocation transition
      starts no provider; the same signal arriving immediately after the transition
      enters the next eligible arbitration batch and triggers bounded cleanup when it
      wins.

      A result settled in an earlier batch ignores later terminal submissions.

      An observation failure after terminal proof preserves that proof and records
      the failure; absent or nonterminal proof at the deadline returns cleanup.

      Verified cleanup preserves the initiating failure. Unverified cleanup returns
      cleanup with complete caller recovery evidence.
    }
  }
}
